<section class="pub-simple-head"><span class="pub-eyebrow">StockChief · Security</span><h1>Data protection policy</h1><p>Effective November 18, 2026 · Version 1.0</p></section>
<section class="pub-legal">
<p>Keeper Inventory LLC operates StockChief at 13 Austra Pkwy, Monroe, NY 10950, United States. The company operator is responsible for this policy and its operational reviews. Contact <a href="mailto:support@stockchiefhq.com">support@stockchiefhq.com</a> for privacy, security or manual-review requests.</p>
<h2 id="purpose">Purpose and customer choices</h2>
<p>We process merchant and customer information only to provide, secure and support the requested StockChief service. We do not sell Shopify customer data or share it for advertising. Merchants control their business records, members, connected providers and permitted workflows. Shopify order processing uses customer names, email and delivery addresses when needed for the order; it does not import customer phone numbers or billing addresses.</p>
<p>A customer may request manual review of a decision, explain their position and contest an overdue-order hold through the merchant or StockChief support. The inventory owner records the choice on the customer’s page under Customer privacy · Credit decisions. Choosing manual review stops future automatic overdue-order holds and releases, and explicitly releases existing holds created by that automatic rule. Holds placed by a person and agreed payment or deposit terms require that person’s review. Support verifies the requester and routes the request to the merchant; it does not decide the customer’s creditworthiness. Manual processing is available on every plan.</p>
<h2 id="retention">Retention schedule</h2>
<p>Keep only what is needed for the service or a documented legal obligation. The company operator reviews retention failures and overdue privacy work weekly, inactive accounts quarterly, and this schedule annually. Merchants should remove unnecessary personal data from active records and review their retention needs at least annually.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Record category</th><th>Period and disposal</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Unapproved import previews</td><td>Cancelled after 14 days. Cancelled import row copies are removed after 14 days; imported business history stays with its inventory.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completed connector working copies and privacy exports</td><td>Daily cleanup minimizes completed feed payloads and removes raw completed webhook payloads and completed privacy exports after 30 days. Open reconciliation issues and unfinished privacy requests defer removal of evidence they need. Review these exceptions weekly and remove the hold when its purpose ends.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completed background-job detail</td><td>Step events expire after 30 days. Minimal jobs, hashes and effect receipts needed to prevent duplicate operations remain with business history.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Per-request resource measurements</td><td>Removed after 100 days. Subscription and accounting evidence is retained separately.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal-data access events</td><td>180 days, followed by daily batch deletion, or earlier with an authorized inventory erasure. Events contain actor identifiers, inventory, category, channel and time, without copying customer content or private URLs. A documented incident or legal requirement may require a separately secured evidence copy until that requirement ends.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active business records, mail and uploaded documents</td><td>While needed for the merchant’s service. At quarterly review, the operator contacts owners of accounts inactive for 12 months to agree export, deletion or a documented continued need. This is a review trigger, not automatic deletion of an active business.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Privacy and offboarding requests</td><td>Target completion within 30 days after verification. Shopify requests have a due date and owner review in Settings → Privacy. Legal holds need a reason and a review date; shared records and unfinished deliveries require resolution before erasure. Required financial amounts and stock history are preserved while covered personal fields are erased. Full offboarding may require a reviewed deletion; a disconnect alone does not delete saved business records.</td></tr>
<tr><td>StockChief subscription receipts, tax and financial records</td><td>Keep the minimum required financial evidence for seven years after the transaction, subject to a documented applicable requirement. Remove unrelated personal content sooner. Merchant accounting records follow the merchant’s instructions and applicable legal retention.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery copies</td><td>Production uses encrypted managed PostgreSQL recovery facilities. Current provider recovery and export windows are up to seven days. Recovery copies expire through that rotation; they are not separately used for business processing. Before resuming external work after a restore, reapply verified deletions, disconnections and access revocations made after the restore point.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Daily cleanup works in bounded batches, so a large backlog may take more than one run. Its completion time and counts are recorded in an operational checkpoint. Unfinished work is retained for resolution and reviewed rather than discarded silently. No deadline in this policy authorizes destruction of records under a valid legal hold.</p>
<h2 id="prevention">Data loss prevention strategy</h2>
<ul><li>Scope access to the authorized inventory and assigned role. Platform support inspection requires a customer-approved, limited, expiring grant. The owner console requires multi-factor authentication.</li>
<li>Use HTTPS and TLS for production database connections. Managed production PostgreSQL storage and backups are encrypted at rest. Connection credentials are additionally encrypted; passwords and API bearer tokens are hashed.</li>
<li>Use isolated generated data for tests. Do not copy live customer data or production credentials into test fixtures, screenshots, recordings or AI prompts. AI-assisted tasks receive only the content needed for the merchant’s enabled workflow.</li>
<li>Keep credentials out of product pages, exports, logs and support messages. Minimize access-event contents, restrict exports to authorized users and deliver privacy exports privately to the verified merchant.</li>
<li>Log authenticated app and API access and supported private customer/supplier links before disclosing their content. Keep separate support-inspection and privacy-export audits. Background work uses job and effect evidence. Access-event logging failure stops the affected app disclosure.</li>
<li>Review operator and support access weekly, investigate unusual exports and revoke unnecessary access promptly. Inventory owners can review their recent access events in Settings → Privacy → Personal data access.</li>
<li>Use reviewed releases, original-provider payment/refund evidence and replay protection. Verify plan limits and existing billing and connections before and after a release. Never substitute a new charge or a second refund for a failed check.</li>
<li>On suspected leakage or loss, follow the response policy below, preserve necessary evidence, contain access and validate recovery before resuming automation.</li></ul>
<h2 id="response">Security incident response policy</h2>
<p>The Keeper Inventory LLC operator is the incident lead and owns assessment, containment, recovery and notification. There is no separate staffed 24-hour response team. Report suspected unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration or loss immediately to the support address above; do not send secrets or full customer records.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Record and assess.</strong> Open a restricted incident record with UTC discovery time, reporter, affected inventories and providers, observed facts, severity, evidence references and the next review time. Treat suspected exposure, active account compromise, destructive corruption and missing security logs as urgent. Distinguish observed facts from hypotheses.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve and contain.</strong> Preserve relevant logs and hashes privately before their expiry. Limit access to the incident lead and specifically engaged responders. Revoke compromised sessions, grants or provider credentials and pause the affected automation or connection as appropriate. Do not delete evidence, disclose secrets in tickets, or issue payments or refunds as a diagnostic action.</li>
<li><strong>Investigate and notify.</strong> Identify affected data, people, merchants, time window and continuing risk. Notify affected merchants without undue delay with known facts and protective steps. For actual or suspected compromise of Shopify merchant data, notify Shopify immediately and no later than 24 hours after awareness, using the Partner support channel required by the current API terms and the published Shopify security-response channel. Assess other contractual, individual and regulatory notices against applicable deadlines; seek qualified assistance where needed. Do not wait for a final root cause before an initial required notice.</li>
<li><strong>Recover safely.</strong> Verify the corrected release or recovery point, integrity of stock and financial records, access grants, deletion/revocation requests and provider synchronization. Reconcile provider evidence to prevent duplicate orders, payments or refunds. Resume only the affected functionality after these checks pass.</li>
<li><strong>Close and improve.</strong> Record the cause, impact, actions and notification timeline, remaining risks and follow-up owners and dates. Preserve incident evidence for 12 months after closure unless a documented obligation requires longer; minimize or remove unrelated personal information. Review lessons after the incident and exercise this procedure at least annually. Scheduled exercises are separate from a proven production restore.</li></ol>
<p>This policy establishes operational responsibilities; it does not claim an independent security certification, completed production restore drill, guaranteed recovery time or approval by Shopify.</p>
